# @NAME@ plugin

This generated C99 **@KIND@** plugin uses the installed Nova-Link SDK.
Required providers: **@REQUIREMENTS@**. It contains no device or network I/O.

```sh
cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=/absolute/path/to/nova-sdk
cmake --build build
ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure
```

The public API is in `include/@PREFIX@_plugin.h`; implement behavior in
`src/@PREFIX@_plugin.c`. Link your application with `@PREFIX@_plugin`, or add this
project with `add_subdirectory()` and set `BUILD_TESTING=OFF` when smoke tests
are not needed.

`examples/smoke.c` shows initialization, manifest startup, polling, and shutdown.
Its dependency providers are **test placeholders**, not implementations of real
services; replace them with the appropriate modules in your application. An
application's transport placeholder accepts sends without transmitting them.
Transport smoke backends also perform no physical I/O.

`examples/conformance.c` adapts this plugin's actual context and factory to the
shared SDK contract runner. It reports exercised and skipped cases explicitly;
passing lifecycle checks does not establish unsupported capabilities or hardware
compatibility. Run it alone with `ctest --test-dir build -V -R '_conformance$'`.
Set `NOVA_PLUGIN_CONFORMANCE=OFF` to retain only the original smoke checks.
The runner needs spare host slots; it defaults off when this starter requires
more than @CONFORMANCE_LIMIT@ providers. Such a full-size manifest remains valid
for runtime use; reduce test-only providers before enabling the conformance test.

@FAULT_README@The normal plugin library links only the runtime SDK. Use the following to
build it without developer-support tests:

```sh
cmake -S . -B build-runtime -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=/absolute/path/to/nova-sdk -DBUILD_TESTING=OFF
cmake --build build-runtime
```

Zero-initialize caller-owned context and `nl_module_instance` storage, initialize
the context, then construct its module descriptor. Keep the context, descriptor
strings, claims, and dependency arrays at stable addresses until shutdown
succeeds. Customize descriptor names and requirements before registration.
Do not initialize or construct another descriptor from a live context. Mutable
contexts belong to one registration; leave `shared_context` disabled.

Use `nl_host_init_plugins()` for the base and `nl_modules_start()` for an
unordered manifest. Dependencies start first. Call `nl_host_poll()` once per
event-loop turn; it polls all plugins before the tick phase. Calls are serialized;
callbacks must return promptly and must not mutate the registry or re-enter
poll/tick. Keep monotonic time in microseconds under application control.

`nl_modules_stop()` may return `NL_ERR_BUSY` after stopping some modules. Keep
all storage alive, drain pending backend work, and retry. Startup rollback bypasses
`can_stop`; cleanup must cancel or drain anything acquired even during a failed
start. A successful send means local transport acceptance, not RF completion or
peer acknowledgement.

The application template is a one-byte latest-value TX/RX example. Replace its
payload format for your protocol; host payloads are limited to 100 bytes and
larger messages require an explicit fragmentation format. The service template
publishes its context through `nl_module_find(host, "@NAME@")->module.service`;
the consumer must know and check the provider interface. The transport template
attaches one send backend and optionally polls input; the backend owns encoding,
device I/O, pending-transfer ownership, and cancellation. Its stop hook must fully
release backend resources even if backend startup failed partway through.
